; Multi-file checks on a public corpus: an entry that requires .jinc files, a
; .jinc on its own, the same .jinc behind an entry and named as the document,
; and a diagnostic that belongs to a required file.
;
; The corpus is not part of this repository, so the stanza runs only when
; JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS names a checkout of it; docs/context-tests.md says
; which one and what the rows expect. The guard is in the stanza rather
; than in the tests: a test that printed a skip line would have to record
; that line as its expected output, and the run that has the corpus would
; then not match it.
;
; The rule writes the value into the build, and the cram stanza depends on
; that file, so dune reruns the tests when the variable changes. Without
; it the value is part of no digest, and a run against another checkout
; would reuse the recorded output of the run before.

(rule
 (target corpus-root.txt)
 (action
  (with-stdout-to
   corpus-root.txt
   (echo %{env:JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS=none}))))

(cram
 (deps
  corpus-root.txt
  corpus.sh
  %{exe:../../lsp-server/native/main.exe})
 (enabled_if
  (<> %{env:JASMIN_CONTEXT_CORPUS=none} "none")))
